// Material families and the vanilla items in them (MaterialCatalog; both sides). See docs/garments.md "Material families".
// Like cooking recipe ingredient categories: a garment part lists the families it takes ("cloth", "pelt"...) or a
// group ("textile" = cloth, leather, pelt; "hard" = wood, metal, bone).
// Other mods extend this by shipping their own <domain>:config/tailoring-materials.json (same format; their
// materials are matched before ours, new families are added, a family already defined keeps its first group), or by
// JSON-patching this file.
// families: code + group (textile | hard; a mod may add a group). "cloth" is built in: patterned actailor:wovencloth
// and actailor:wovenlinen are always cloth and not listed (their pattern shows, movable).
// materials: code = item or block code, wildcards allowed; the FIRST matching entry wins (put specific codes before
// wildcards). family: one of the families. A filled part consumes 1 item.
// texture: vanilla texture the part shows (the item's own, from its itemtype); {*} = what the code's * matched
// (game:plank-* on plank-oak → oak). Server items carry no textures, so they are named here; the smoke errors on a
// matched item whose texture asset does not exist. Textures tile 1:1 under the part (no scaling), rotation only.
// crop: [x1, y1, x2, y2] texels of that PNG tiled 1:1 under the part (null = whole texture); species pelts crop the
// animal's back from the entity texture (scripts/pelt-regions.sh = largest fur face of the vanilla pelt item shape).
// texels: garment texels one item covers. Unused since lane/garment-system (1 item per part), kept for a later area cost.
// color: flat stand-in when the texture cannot be loaded.
{
	"families": [
		{ "code": "cloth", "group": "textile" },
		{ "code": "leather", "group": "textile" },
		{ "code": "pelt", "group": "textile" },
		{ "code": "wood", "group": "hard" },
		{ "code": "metal", "group": "hard" },
		{ "code": "bone", "group": "hard" }
	],
	"materials": [
